WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.499
Authorization decides what an
authenticated subject may access or do.

2
00:00:06.579 --> 00:00:11.979
Django permissions and groups can provide
a useful foundation,

3
00:00:12.059 --> 00:00:19.358
especially for broad capabilities,
administrative workflows, and reusable checks.

4
00:00:19.438 --> 00:00:24.937
But many real applications need more than
role-level decisions.

5
00:00:25.017 --> 00:00:31.716
They need object-level authorization,
tenant boundaries, ownership checks,

6
00:00:31.796 --> 00:00:38.696
workflow state checks, and context-aware
decisions that account for the current

7
00:00:38.776 --> 00:00:42.375
user, action, resource, and environment.

8
00:00:42.455 --> 00:00:49.654
Object-level authorization is critical
when views accept identifiers for records,

9
00:00:49.734 --> 00:00:56.034
files, projects, organizations, invoices,
tickets, or other resources.

10
00:00:56.114 --> 00:01:02.413
A user being logged in does not mean they
can access every object with a valid

11
00:01:02.493 --> 00:01:03.593
primary key.

12
00:01:03.673 --> 00:01:10.572
The server must verify that the caller has
permission for the specific resource in

13
00:01:10.652 --> 00:01:12.452
the current context.

14
00:01:12.532 --> 00:01:18.031
Template logic that hides a button is
useful for user experience,

15
00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:20.711
but it is not an access control.

16
00:01:20.791 --> 00:01:25.290
A manually crafted request still reaches
the server.

17
00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:32.569
The Django admin deserves careful
protection because it can expose high-impact data

18
00:01:32.649 --> 00:01:33.749
and actions.

19
00:01:33.829 --> 00:01:40.328
Staff and superuser privileges should be
limited, reviewed, and monitored.

20
00:01:40.408 --> 00:01:45.208
Administrative actions should follow least
privilege,

21
00:01:45.288 --> 00:01:50.387
and admin exposure should match the risk
of the environment.

22
00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:54.267
The admin can be a powerful operational
tool,

23
00:01:54.347 --> 00:02:01.446
but it should not become an unreviewed
backdoor around application authorization,

24
00:02:01.526 --> 00:02:04.926
approval flows, or audit expectations.

25
00:02:05.006 --> 00:02:12.005
Reusable permission checks can improve
consistency when they are easy to apply and

26
00:02:12.085 --> 00:02:13.385
hard to forget.

27
00:02:13.465 --> 00:02:18.164
Decorators, mixins, service-layer guards,
managers,

28
00:02:18.244 --> 00:02:23.543
and explicit helper functions can all
support authorization.

29
00:02:23.623 --> 00:02:30.723
The important question is whether
enforcement happens server-side and close to the

30
00:02:30.803 --> 00:02:32.402
sensitive action.

31
00:02:32.482 --> 00:02:36.782
Mature Django teams test authorization
directly,

32
00:02:36.862 --> 00:02:44.061
including denied cases, cross-tenant
cases, ownership changes, admin boundaries,

33
00:02:44.141 --> 00:02:50.540
and paths that are easy to overlook, such
as exports, imports, bulk actions,

34
00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:52.420
and background work.
